    "fullText": "",
    "fullTextFormats": [],
    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "Add §3204-a, amd §3604, Ed L",
    "lawSection": "Education Law",
    "memo": " BILL NUMBER:  S6747\n\n TITLE OF BILL :  An act to amend the education law, in relation to\nestablishing the St. Patrick's Day school holiday\n\n PURPOSE : To establish St. Patrick's Day as a school holiday for all\ncity school districts of one million or more with an Irish-American\npopulation of 7.5 percent or more.\n\n SUMMARY OF PROVISIONS :\n\nSection 1 of this bill amends the Education Law by adding a new\nsection 3204-a, which provides for a school holiday for St. Patrick's\nDay in those districts where the city has a population of one million\nor more and an Irish-American population equal to or greater than 7.5%\ndetermined by the census.\n\nSection 2 of this bill adds a new subdivision 7-c to the Education\nLaw, which states that the Education Commissioner shall disregard any\nreduction in attendance resulting from observance of St. Patrick's Day\nand may be counted as a session day if necessary.\n\nSection 3 provides that this act shall take effect on the one hundred\ntwentieth day after it shall have become a law; provided that any and\nall rules and regulations and any other measures necessary to\nimplement any provisions of this act on its effective date may be\npromulgated and taken, respectively, on or before such effective date.\n\n JUSTIFICATION :  St. Patrick's Day is the oldest cultural celebration\nin the Irish-American community. It is a traditional time of reunion\nand celebration with family and friends, as well as a holy day of\nobligation for Irish Catholics. However, public schools do not close\nfor that day and education authorities have even gone so far as to use\nthe evening of this day for parent-teacher conferences. This places an\nunfair burden on school children, parents, and teachers within the\nIrish-American community. It is impractical to keep public schools\nopen in Irish-American communities when there is a significant number\nof students and instructors who take the day off.\n\nIn 2014, the Governor approved legislation (Chapter 491) directing\nschool districts to determine whether school session should be held on\na day where a religious or cultural holiday would lead to a waste of\neducational resources due to significant absenteeism. Considering that\na large Irish-American population in New York City is impacted by St.\nPatrick's Day, schools should be closed for the observance of this\nholiday, or if school is in session, a child's inability to attend\nschool on this holiday should be disregarded.\n\n LEGISLATIVE HISTORY : New bill\n\n FISCAL IMPLICATIONS : To be determined.\n\n EFFECTIVE DATE :  This act shall take effect on the one hundred\ntwentieth day after it shall have become a law; provided that any and\nall rules and regulations and any other measures necessary to\nimplement any provisions of this act on its effective date may be\npromulgated and taken, respectively, on or before such effective date.\n",
